Good. You are *exactly* at the point where GitHub stops being “version control” and becomes **the spine of your lab’s memory**.

This is the moment to introduce it — *before* you port Stage A/B — otherwise you’ll fossilise chaos.

Here is the precise policy.

---

# What GitHub is in this lab

GitHub is **not**:

• a place to dump code
• a place to back up data
• a place to experiment messily

GitHub **is**:

> The immutable specification of how your laboratory works.

Everything in GitHub must be **rebuildable from scratch** except the frozen evidence living on WD4/WD8/HPC.

---

# What goes into GitHub (and what never does)

### Goes into GitHub

```
Composer/
Orchestra/           # all stage code + wrappers
scripts/
configs/
docs/
BuildingPlan/
run_schema.yaml
README.md
```

### Never goes into GitHub

```
Orchestra/stageC_archive/views/runs/    # symlinked evidence
Orchestra/stageD_reduce/cache/
Orchestra/stageD_reduce/outputs/
WD4 / WD8 / HPC archives
Any prod.nc, trajectories, parquet, logs
```

GitHub is the *control plane*, never the freezer.

---

# When to initialise GitHub

**Now. Before porting Stage A.**

Because Stage A is the first piece of irreversible knowledge you are about to transplant.

---

# Exact bootstrap procedure (do this once)

From inside `~/WORK/MSE_LAB`:

```bash
git init
git branch -M main
```

Create `.gitignore` (critical):

```gitignore
# --- DATA / EVIDENCE ---
Orchestra/stageC_archive/views/runs/
Orchestra/stageD_reduce/cache/
Orchestra/stageD_reduce/outputs/

**/*.nc
**/*.rst
**/*.out
**/*.parquet
**/*.csv
**/*.log
**/*.pdb
**/*.prmtop
**/*.inpcrd
**/*.mdcrd
**/*.dcd
**/*.xtc

# builder/sim scratch
**/work/
**/builds/
**/__pycache__/
```

Now make the **Genesis commit**:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Genesis: establish MSE_LAB control plane"
```

This commit is sacred. Never rewrite it.

---

# GitHub usage rules (non-negotiable)

You only commit when you create **a new lab capability**:

| You do this                  | You commit |
| ---------------------------- | ---------- |
| Add Stage A builder          | YES        |
| Wrap Stage B local runner    | YES        |
| Change run_manifest schema   | YES        |
| Change run_schema.yaml       | YES        |
| Change Composer ledger logic | YES        |
| Run a simulation             | NO         |
| Generate parquet tables      | NO         |
| Run rebuild_all              | NO         |
| Fix a broken run             | NO         |

Commits represent **new physical capabilities of the lab**, not experiments.

---

# Branch policy

You only need two:

• `main` – reality
• `dev` – where you port things

Today:

```bash
git checkout -b dev
```

You work on `dev`.
Only merge into `main` when a stage *actually works*.

---

# Why this matters

From now on:

> You are no longer building a project.
> You are building a **machine that makes science**.

GitHub becomes the blueprint of that machine.

WD8 holds the fossils.
GitHub holds the laws of physics.

---

Now:

1. Initialise git
2. Add `.gitignore`
3. Make the Genesis commit
4. Create `dev` branch

Then you may start porting Stage A.

Once you do that, paste the old Stage A folder tree and entrypoint command and we wire it cleanly into Orchestra.

